\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Diagnostic and therapeutic errors remain leading contributors to preventable morbidity and mortality, causing an estimated 80,000 hospital deaths annually in the United States alone and compromising 5\% to 15\% of clinical encounters. The vast majority of these adverse events stem from systematic human cognitive vulnerability (dysrationalia, heuristic biases, and premature closure) paired with uncalibrated, non-Bayesian probabilistic reasoning. This paper introduces an integrated, mathematically rigorous, and cognitively debiased clinical workflow. Synthesizing formal decision-tree utility analysis (Pauker-Kassirer threshold models; Casazza et al.) with dual-process metacognitive forcing functions (Croskerry), we formulate a 10-stage operational architecture. The model establishes quantitative gating parameters across the clinical continuum: defining mutually exclusive hypothesis sets ($\sum p_i = 1.0$) with mandatory residual allocation, enforcing formal testing thresholds ($p_A$, $p_T$), eradicating non-Bayesian indiscriminate testing (the ``Casablanca strategy''), calculating expected utility ($E[U]$), tracking longitudinal rate kinetics ($\Delta \text{Parameter}/\Delta t$), and executing rule-governed disposition pathways (Medically Fit for Discharge [MFD], specialized escalation, or closed iterative loop re-entry). This architecture provides a scalable framework to optimize diagnostic accuracy, streamline testing resource stewardship, and elevate clinical patient safety.
\end{abstract}

\vspace{1em}
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Clinical Decision-Making, Bayesian Probability, Pauker-Kassirer Thresholds, Dual-Process Theory, Cognitive Debiasing, Diagnostic Error, Health Utility.

