\section{Discussion and Clinical Translation}

The integration of formal threshold mathematics with cognitive forcing functions closes a critical gap in contemporary medical training. While clinical prediction rules and electronic alerting systems offer valuable data, their utility degrades when clinicians fail to interpret test characteristics through a formal Bayesian lens or succumb to cognitive anchoring. 

By hardcoding the rule $\sum p_i = 1.0$ with a mandatory allocation for an ``Other/Unexpected'' bucket, this framework systematically counters diagnostic omission bias—the primary origin of catastrophic diagnostic misses. Furthermore, calculating explicit $p_A$ and $p_T$ limits eliminates defensive medicine habits and testing overuse, safeguarding clinical environments against iatrogenic harm and diagnostic inflation.

\section{Conclusion}

The proposed 10-stage decision framework harmonizes quantitative decision analysis with human cognitive debiasing. Applying these operational checks across hospital admissions, diagnostic workups, and therapeutic tracking transforms clinical decision-making into an audited, reproducible, and patient-centered scientific discipline.

